---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { places } from "../../data/deployments";

const rows = [
  {
    where: "Hosted app or backend",
    identity:
      "A registered confidential client, and the cloud’s identity for the workload",
    user: "Authorization code with PKCE: one grant per user, kept on the server",
    secrets:
      "Refresh tokens encrypted in your database; the rest in a secrets manager",
    risk: "Every user’s tokens in one place; fetching a token for the wrong user",
  },
  {
    where: "Kubernetes",
    identity: "ServiceAccount token, pod certificate or SPIFFE ID",
    user: "Token exchange at a gateway or in the agent",
    secrets: "With federation, none long-lived; otherwise a Secret",
    risk: "Shared service accounts; loose federation trust rules",
  },
  {
    where: "Managed platform",
    identity: "A platform-issued agent identity",
    user: "The platform’s token vault and exchange",
    secrets: "In the platform’s vault",
    risk: "Opaque defaults; knowing what the platform can do as your agent",
  },
  {
    where: "Desktop, IDE, CLI",
    identity: "None of its own: a public OAuth client",
    user: "Authorization code with PKCE, as the signed-in user",
    secrets: "Operating system keychain",
    risk: "Tokens on disk; any local process can claim a localhost redirect",
  },
  {
    where: "CI pipeline",
    identity: "The CI system’s OIDC token for the job",
    user: "Usually none: the job acts as itself",
    secrets: "With federation, none long-lived",
    risk: "Trust rules that match more repositories or branches than intended",
  },
  {
    where: "Sandbox",
    identity: "Whatever the host gives it, ideally nothing reusable",
    user: "Held outside; injected per request",
    secrets: "Outside the sandbox",
    risk: "Misuse of access while the sandbox runs",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Agent deployments: where identity comes from"
  description="Where an agent runs decides where its identity comes from. Kubernetes, managed agent platforms, local machines, CI and sandboxes compared."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Deployments</p>
      <h1>Where the agent runs decides where its identity comes from</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The flows are the same everywhere. What changes with the runtime is the starting credential: what the platform
        can vouch for, where a secret could safely live, and who can see the agent’s traffic.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid two">
        {
          places.map((p) => (
            <a class="card" href={p.href}>
              <h2>{p.title}</h2>
              <p>{p.blurb}</p>
              <div class="chips">
                {p.chips.map((c) => (
                  <span class="chip">{c}</span>
                ))}
              </div>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Side by side</h2>
        <p>The same four questions asked of each runtime.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Deployments compared">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Runtime</th>
              <th>The agent’s own identity</th>
              <th>Acting for a user</th>
              <th>Where secrets live</th>
              <th>Main risk</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>{r.where}</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>{r.identity}</td>
                  <td>{r.user}</td>
                  <td>{r.secrets}</td>
                  <td>{r.risk}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p class="callout-title">A pattern across all of them</p>
        <p>
          In every runtime the strongest option is the one where the platform attests to the agent and nothing
          long-lived is stored: a projected token, a job’s OIDC token, a platform-issued agent identity. Where that is
          not available, the next best is a secret the agent can use but cannot read.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  td {
    min-width: 11rem;
  }
  td:first-child {
    min-width: 8rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
